        "original_id": "b5b7a36a-3d40-52e6-843d-e9eb01884ff7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Antelope, Kansas; North America; interior western and central North America; Lincolnville; Lost Springs; Pilsen; Tampa; Ramona; Burdick; Marion County; Morris County; Dickinson County; Chase County\n- Animals: Pronghorn; antelope; true antelopes\n- Organizations: Centre USD 397; Centre High School\n- Concepts: parallel evolution; ecological niche\n\nFacts:\n- Antelope, Kansas, was named after antelope grazing near the site where the first school was being built.\n- The pronghorn, Antilocapra americana, is an artiodactyl mammal indigenous to interior western and central North America.\n- The pronghorn is not a true antelope, but in North America it is often called the American antelope, prong buck, pronghorn antelope, or simply antelope because it resembles Old World antelopes and fills a similar ecological niche through parallel evolution.\n- Centre USD 397 includes Lincolnville, Lost Springs, Pilsen, Tampa, Ramona, Antelope, Burdick, and nearby rural areas in Marion, Morris, Dickinson, and Chase counties.\n- Centre High School is the only high school in Centre USD 397.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
